  "worked_solution": "To plot $x=-{y}^{2}-\\frac{8y}{5},$ we will first plot the function $y=-{x}^{2}-\\frac{8x}{5}$ and then reflect it over the line $y=x.$\n\nThe curve $y=-{x}^{2}-\\frac{8x}{5}$ is a parabola. It has the following factored form:\n\n$$\ny=-x(x+\\frac{8}{5})\n$$\n\nFrom the factored form above, we get the following graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240562/assets/26a35ba2e93c08af12f916f16ad40f46a8ea0242e23bc516d279a015094db872.png)\n\nNow, to get the curve $x=-{y}^{2}-\\frac{8y}{5},$ we just have to reflect the curve $y=-{x}^{2}-\\frac{8x}{5}$ over the line $y=x,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240562/assets/049683350600f401aa18feb6d5ea5662965ba0740f4b23a524e93e40f61f0bc4.png)",
